<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>Field-level provenance transforms data extraction from a black box into an auditable, debuggable, and verifiable system. The core idea is simple: every extracted value carries a structured record of where it came from, how it was extracted, and how confident the system is that it is correct.</p>
<p>The trade-off is straightforward. Full provenance costs some latency and noticeably more storage per record. What it buys is the ability to answer "why is this value wrong?" from the record itself, instead of by reproducing the extraction by hand.</p>
<p>The regulatory landscape is moving in the same direction. The EU AI Act, GDPR Article 22, and ISO/IEC 42001 all require documented evidence of data origin and processing. Field-level provenance is the technical mechanism that satisfies these requirements.</p>
<p>Start small. Pick one extraction pipeline, add provenance tracking for the most critical fields, and measure the impact on debugging time. If you use the Ollagraph API for fetching, keep the provenance layer in your own code: record the URL you requested, the fetch time, and each field's raw source text as you build the record. The API returns page content and embedded structured data; it does not return provenance envelopes.</p>
